# PhyNetPy Code Documentation Rules

When I create an agent to review code or create code, these rules for commenting,
documenting, and writing code apply!

## Inline/Functional comments

For writing comments that explain what a piece of code does, it MUST follow these guildlines.

#### Every function/control flow block that has over 5 lines of code must contain an inline comment

Comments should be concise, but sufficiently written such that a human can read
the comments that apply for a given block of code and understand what it does at 
a high level.

#### Comments should be of high quality
A BAD comment example: 
    "#Compute the result"

This is vague, and offers no real insight as to what is going on.

A GOOD comment example:
    "#The value of gamma is set to .7 here due to formula 3.2 from web source 3 (cited at top of file)"

This is specific, explains WHY a line of code is the way it is, and offers reproducible knowledge that a human can track down. 

#### Comments should explain high complexity logic

Do not write a comment for a line of code that is fairly easy to understand. 
Use a comment when the operation or task for that line of code is complex, complicated, and needs explaining.

## Doc Strings

Each docstring should be descriptive of the method/helper function/class. Docstrings
must be written for every class and function, regardless of whether it is or is not
user facing.

#### Doc String Structure.

Each docstring should contain a description, sources (if academic sources are used to
formulate what the class/function does), and finally, a list of all inputs, outputs, and 
exceptions that are raised on any fatal error (and what that error would be).

#### Example

For a function that computes a matrix from a few parameters:

def compute_P_matrix(alpha : float, beta : float, dim : tuple[int, int]) -> np.ndarray:
    """
    This function computes and returns the "P matrix". A P matrix is a matrix defined by 
    two parameters, alpha and beta. Alpha and Beta are ... blah blah blah. Also, a matrix size is
    defined by dim, a tuple containing row and column counts, respectively.

    This P matrix is defined in a paper by .... blah blah blah. It is used specifically for ...blah blah.

    Args:
        alpha (float) : blah blah blah
        beta (float) : blah blah blah
        dim (tuple[int, int]): blah blah blah
    Returns:
        np.ndarray : blah blah blah
    Raises:
        ValueError: when blah blah blah happens.
    """

    <insert function code here>

Each docstring should follow this exact structure. For Classes, Returns/Raises may be 
excluded, but the Args section should list any class constructors and what parameters they take, instead.









    
